course: dictionary
lesson: 1066
title: Boulder: a rock too big to lift
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A boulder is a very large, rounded rock, too big to pick up. Read the words around it to check. «Imagine a boulder as big as a car sitting alone in a field» tells you it is huge, because it is as big as a car.
explain: A boulder is much bigger than a pebble. A pebble fits in your hand, but a boulder does not. Over many years, weathering and erosion slowly break boulders into smaller stones, pebbles and sand.
explain: Context also tells you what boulders do. «A glacier can carry huge boulders far from where they broke off» shows that boulders break off from rock, and that ice can carry them a long way. Check your meaning by swapping it in: "A glacier can carry huge, very large rounded rocks far from where they broke off" still makes sense.
fact: A boulder is a very large, rounded rock, too big to pick up.
fact: «Erratics» are boulders that glaciers dropped far from the rocks they came from.
fact: Glaciers, rivers and landslides can all move boulders.
fact: Weathering and erosion slowly break boulders into smaller stones, pebbles and sand.
fact: «Bouldering» is climbing on low rocks or walls, often with a mat below you to land on.
exercise: «A glacier can carry huge boulders far from where they broke off.» What is a boulder? | A kind of sand found on beaches | *A very large, rounded rock, too big to pick up | A small stone that fits in your hand
exercise: A sign on a mountain road warns of falling boulders. What does the sign mean? | *Watch out, rocks may fall from the hill above | Speed up to get past quickly | The road is closed for a picnic | Drivers should pick up the rocks
exercise: «Glaciers dropped huge boulders, called erratics, far from the rocks they came from.» Which of these describes erratics? | Rivers that flow uphill | *Boulders dropped by a glacier far from where they started | Tiny grains of sand on a beach | Small pebbles you can hold in your hand
test: Which process slowly breaks boulders into smaller stones, pebbles and sand? | Bouldering | *Weathering and erosion | Climbing on a mat | A landslide sign
test: In some stories, what do heroes roll away from cave mouths? | Sand | Clouds | Leaves | *Boulders
exam: Which sentence is true about a boulder compared with a pebble? | A boulder and a pebble are always the same size | A boulder is always made of sand | *A boulder is much bigger, too big to pick up, while a pebble fits in your hand | A pebble is bigger than a boulder
